    "lawCode": "Amd §2500-a, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4316\n\nSPONSOR: RIVERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to newborn screening\nfor adrenoleukodystrophy and glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficien-\ncy\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL: To add\n\nglucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency to the list of conditions\nthat all newborns are screened for.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends Public Health Law § 2500-a to add glucose-6-phosphate\ndehydrogenase deficiency to the list of conditions that all newborns are\nscreened for. This section also includes technical cleanup to make the\nsection clearer and format it in line with the rest of the Public Health\nLaw. It removes subdivision 2 paragraph (c), which required the Deparm-\ntent of Health to add testing for adrenoleukodystrophy after conducting\na validation process, because the Wadsworth Center has already completed\nthat validation process, so it adds adrenoleukodystrophy to the screen-\ning list.\n\n§ 2. Takes effect 180 days after it becomes a law. Effective immediate-\nly, the Health Commissioner shall make regulations and take other\nactions reasonably necessary to implement this act on that date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nGlucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency is an inherited\ngenetic enzyme disorder that affects the functioning of red blood cells\nand has the potential to cause attacks of acute hemolytic anemia, severe\nneonatal jaundice that can lead to hyperbilirubinemia (a high concen-\ntration of bilirubin in the blood) and kernicterus (where the bilirubin\ncrosses the blood-brain barrier and affects the developing brain), and,\nmore rarely, chronic anemia called chronic non-spherocytic hemolytic\nanemia (CNSHA). In the most severe cases, untreated G6PD deficiency\nleads to permanent brain damage and sometimes death. It also weakens the\nimmune system, and recent studies suggest it leaves those affected more\nvulnerable to COVID-19.\n\nIf a newborn is known to have G6PD deficiency, the newborn can be\nchecked for bilirubin before it reaches harmful levels, which often\noccurs after the newborn leaves the hospital but before the first pedia-\ntrician visit. Certain foods and drugs are known to trigger symptoms of\nG6PD, and the simplest treatment is avoiding them. But since screening\nfor G6PD deficiency is not common practice and most carriers do not know\nthey carry it, families and health care providers are often unaware of\nthe triggers and do not consider checking bilirubin levels until damage\nhas already been done.  Prevalence of the deficiency is highest in popu-\nlations from suk;Saharan Africa, the Mediterranean, and Southeast Asia.\nIt is thought to occur at a rateabetWeen 4-7% in the United States, and\nthe World Health Organization (WHO) says that any genetic disorder with\na prevalence of above 3% in the population should be universally\nscreened. The Newborn Screening and Molecular Biology Branch of the CDC\nprovides technical laboratory support for state newborn screening\nprograms and has an existing technical program to support screening for\nG6PD deficiency. A state as ethnically diverse as New York should be\nscreening all newborns for G6PD deficiency to ensure that newborns with\nthe condition receive the care they need and avoid potentially lethal\ncomplications.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNegligible. Considerable savings from avoiding costly treatment and care\nif the condition is identified early.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the one hundred eightieth day after it\nshall have become a law. Effective immediately, the commissioner of\nhealth shall make regulations and take other actions reasonably neces-\nsary to implement this act on that date.\n",
